# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever cloned.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is the Windows default and it checks text files out
# with CRLF. That is right for a file Notepad opens and wrong for a file
# Linux executes: `docker-entrypoint.sh` starting `#!/bin/sh\r` makes the
# kernel look for an interpreter literally named `sh\r`, and the container
# exits before a single line of the app runs. The image built fine from the
# working copy it was written in and would have broken on the next clone.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reason, one step removed: a `RUN` with backslash continuations is a
# shell script by the time it executes.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf

# Read by Linux tooling, and YAML is whitespace-significant enough without
# stray carriage returns in it.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# PowerShell runs on Windows and CRLF is what an editor there expects. The
# `.tmpl` is served as a download and run by double-click, so it wants the
# native endings too.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.ps1.tmpl text eol=crlf

# Binary, so never touched.
*.xlsx binary
*.xlsm binary
*.pptx binary
*.docx binary
*.pdf binary
*.7z binary
*.zip binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.db binary
